<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/expert/film-theory-and-criticism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Film theory is the <strong>literature lesson\'s theory, in motion</strong> — realism vs
    formalism, the gaze, genre contracts, authorship — plus the questions film uniquely forces:
    what does the machine itself do to the watching mind? This is the ladder\'s summit: you can
    no longer watch a film naively, which is the point.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="apparatus, gaze, genre/authorship, the discipline now" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />

  <h2>Writing film criticism well</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Describe, then argue:</strong> the critique lesson\'s Feldman order — quote the frame (describe the shot), then make the claim; every aesthetic judgement should point at something visible (mise-en-scène analysis is the critic\'s evidence kit).</li>
    <li><strong>Take the film\'s own ambitions seriously:</strong> judge the film it tried to be (a slow film isn\'t "boring" because it\'s slow — that\'s sometimes the argument); the fairness principle is the same fairness the journalism lesson demands of a profile.</li>
    <li><strong>Criticism is writing, under review:</strong> the best criticism is an essay worth reading without the film (Kael, Sontag, Farber) — the literature lesson\'s craft applied to argument; negative reviews still carry the duty of accuracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Where theory meets practice:</strong> every film-maker reads (or embodies) theory — the next generation\'s grammar lesson is the last generation\'s theory essay. The loop the whole film ladder has been drawing, closed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bazin, What Is Cinema?; Mulvey, "Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema" (1975); Bordwell, Post-Theory; the literature lesson\'s theory chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
